title A duplicate answers coverage as well as a unique row does
prompt The standing baton -- open the lap, take the next agent-doable work, reds first.
rota lap 4136, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels. Law and boundary; its sense is touch, and you cannot touch a thing's middle, only the surface where it stops. Every row of today's shelf had already read a row, so N mod 5 stood. What it surfaced, twice. First, the boundary this lap needed was not between right and wrong rows but between a row OPENING and a note about one, and that boundary is written on the line itself. Second, and this is the row's own instruction -- press on a boundary and see whether the hand passes through -- I pressed on my own advice line and it gave way: the scan told a reader to drop %512's redundant copy, and %512's two shelves are deliberate and say so on their faces.

think Reds first, and %536 was my own row from last lap. Its repair has two halves and only one is mine: which of the two %530 rows moves is a word Keaton owns, since both numbers are published. The other half I named and did not take -- a duplicate_numbers reading in the monotone scan, so the next doubling reds inside the tree that made it rather than in the next ship to fetch. That is the crux, it is Lindy, and it was sitting written down.
think The obvious implementation is a trap and I nearly walked into it. Keyed on the number alone, cut -f1 | uniq -d, the live ledger reads 59 doubled numbers. Fifty-seven of them are lawful: a closure note speaks about its row and keeps its number, and the scan's own comment says so twenty lines above where I was about to write. A gate reading 59 would refuse a ledger that is perfectly whole, which is REDS %100, and it would be my own last lap's red -- an operator is not a claim -- committed a second time in a different room.
think So I measured three keys before choosing one. Number alone: 59. (number, stamp): 15, because a closure note carries its own later stamp. (number, stamp) off row OPENINGS only, where an opening is the sigil, the number, then IMMEDIATELY a parenthesised stamp: exactly two, and both are real. Zero false positives across 447 openings.
think The narrow shape does two jobs at once, which is why it is the right one rather than merely a working one. It excludes the prose mention that happens to open a line -- **REDS %155**, which found the same breach -- and it hands the row its true key, since derived-spine says a row's immutable identity is its one-clock stamp and the %N beside it is a view. The discriminator is not a heuristic about content; it is the ledger's own grammar.
think The two things it finds are different things, so they are named apart. %530 is two incidents under one number and wants a word on which renumbers. %512 is ONE incident written into two shelves under one stamp -- and that one turned out to be deliberate, which the detail line now says rather than prescribing a repair. %536's own lesson warns that two readings firing together are one reading in two names -- these never fire together, and a leg proves that by planting one of each and reading one in each column.
think Ceilings that only fall rather than gates at zero, for the reason duplicate_headlines already carries: both instances stand on published shelves, so resolving either edits testimony and is Keaton's word under debride. A gate at zero would red on the tree as it honestly stands, and a wall that reds on the ordinary is a wall somebody turns off.
think The welcome is the load-bearing leg, so I proved it from the other side rather than asserting it. plant_write copies the scan into a pen and strips one thing -- the space-then-paren that says the stamp follows the number immediately -- and the copy then reads the closure-note pen as three openings with %1 double-bound. Using plant.sh means a lap that moves that line is told plant_matched_nothing rather than handed an unmutated copy that passes, which is %519 and %535 applied to my own work on the lap after I wrote the law.

obs The stripped copy still answers verdict=ok, and that is the sharper finding. A loosened regex does not announce itself; it invents a phantom double-binding that sits AT the ceiling and silently spends the tree's one unit of slack. Without the welcome leg the loosening would surface later as a mystery -- a ceiling that will not fall for no reason anyone can name.
obs The new reading and reds_spine_derive now find the same %530 from two directions, one inside the tree and one against the remote. That agreement is the point of the lap: the remote reading arrived eighty minutes after the collision landed, in a different ship.
obs A meter that cannot see what it measures answers zero, and zero is also what a whole ledger answers. So the scan prints bindings= and the witness holds a floor of 400 under the 447 measured today -- well below ordinary growth, high enough that a reading going dark reds rather than reporting virtue.
obs The living pin went 301 bytes over bound when the repair clause landed. No CLOSED row stood on it -- every one had already folded -- so I folded %535, my own BOOKED row from last lap, which is exactly what door B seated the fold for. The pin came back to 37,489 with 3,471 of slack, and the card to 40,903 with 57.
obs The correction I caught before the send is the better half of the lap. The scan's detail line for %512 read drop the redundant copy, and I had written that from the shape of the finding rather than from the pages. Opening them, the later shelf's own head says This row has two shelves, and the reason is the ledger's own standing question -- two hands folded one row without knowing, both pages were kept, one carrying what the row taught and one how it ended. So the reading counts and names; it does not prescribe. A meter that reads a shape and reports a purpose has invented the purpose, which is exactly the sentence my own %535 was written to hold, and I nearly shipped it again one room over.
obs The header both files carry has claimed 1..N with NO GAPS AND NO DUPLICATES since 20260730, and the code deduped the row numbers with sort -u before the gap walk -- so the walk could never see a duplicate. The comment was not describing the code; it was describing an intention. That claim is true for the first time as of this lap. It is the air row's own reading: the boundary was a wish, and the hand went straight through it.

obs %440 fired again on the send, which makes twelve across five laps. The rebase auto-merged a peer's shelf row one position low -- 20260907.011322 under 20260907.011253 rather than above it -- and the row bound guard is GREEN either way, since it reads bytes per row rather than order. Sorted by hand in this commit, 2 rows moved of 14. The dedupe-and-sort is mechanical and the same every time, which is what a loom is for.

loom guard=reds_ledger_monotone bindings=447 rows=536 numbers_double_bound=1 rows_double_shelved=1 legs_added=11
loom key=number doubled_numbers=59 key=number_stamp doubled=15 key=row_opening doubled=2 false_positives=0
loom strip=opening_shape bindings_before=2 bindings_after=3 numbers_double_bound_before=0 numbers_double_bound_after=1 verdict_after=ok
loom pin=construction/REDS.md bytes_before=39260 bytes_over=301 bytes_after=37489 bound=40960 rows_folded=1
loom pin=construction/ITINERARY.md bytes_after=40903 bound=40960 slack=57

